#: ../../../completers.rst:5
msgid "Tab-Completion"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:8
msgid "Overview"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:10
msgid ""
"As with many other shells, xonsh ships with the ability to complete "
"partially-specified arguments upon hitting the \"tab\" key."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:13
msgid ""
"In Python-mode, pressing the \"tab\" key will complete based on the variable "
"names in the current builtins, globals, and locals, as well as xonsh "
"language keywords & operators, files & directories, and environment variable "
"names. In subprocess-mode, xonsh additionally completes based on the names "
"of any executable files on your $PATH, alias keys, and full Bash completion "
"for the commands themselves."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:20
msgid ""
"xonsh also provides a mechanism by which the results of a tab completion can "
"be customized (i.e., new completions can be generated, or a subset of the "
"built-in completions can be ignored)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:24
msgid ""
"This page details the internal structure of xonsh's completion system and "
"includes instructions for implementing new tab completion functions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:29
msgid "Structure"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:31
msgid ""
"xonsh's built-in completers live in the ``xonsh.completers`` package, and "
"they are managed through an instance of ``OrderedDict`` (``__xonsh__."
"completers``) that maps unique identifiers to completion functions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:35
msgid ""
"The completers are divided to **exclusive** completers and **non-exclusive** "
"completers. Non-exclusive completers are used for completions that are "
"relevant but don't cover the whole completions needed (e.g. completions for "
"the built-in commands ``and``/``or``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:39
msgid ""
"When the \"tab\" key is pressed, xonsh loops over the completion functions "
"in order, calling each one in turn and collecting its output until it "
"reaches an **exclusive** one that returns a non-empty set of completions for "
"the current line. The collected completions are then displayed to the user."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:46
msgid "Listing Active Completers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:48
msgid ""
"A list of the active completers can be viewed by running the ``completer "
"list`` command.  This command will display names and descriptions of the "
"currently-active completers, in the order in which they will be checked."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:55
msgid "Writing a New Completer"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:57
msgid ""
"Completers are implemented as Python functions that take a :class:"
"`Completion Context <xonsh.parsers.completion_context.CompletionContext>` "
"object. Examples for the context object:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:60
msgid ""
"# ls /tmp/<TAB>\n"
"CompletionContext(\n"
"    command=CommandContext(\n"
"        args=(CommandArg(value='ls'),),\n"
"        arg_index=1, prefix='/tmp/',\n"
"        ),\n"
"    python=PythonContext(multiline_code=\"ls /tmp/\", cursor_index=8, "
"ctx={...})\n"
")\n"
"\n"
"# ls $(whic<TAB> \"python\") -l\n"
"CompletionContext(\n"
"    command=CommandContext(\n"
"        args=(CommandArg(value='python', opening_quote='\"', "
"closing_quote='\"'),),\n"
"        arg_index=0, prefix='whic', subcmd_opening='$(',\n"
"    ),\n"
"    python=None\n"
")\n"
"\n"
"# echo @(sys.exe<TAB>)\n"
"CompletionContext(\n"
"    command=None,\n"
"    python=PythonContext(\n"
"        multiline_code=\"sys.exe\", cursor_index=7,\n"
"        is_sub_expression=True, ctx={...},\n"
"    )\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:90
msgid ""
"Xonsh still supports legacy completers - see `Legacy Completers Support`_. "
"For backwards-compatibility, contextual completers need to be marked (as "
"seen in the examples)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:93
msgid ""
"This function should return a python set of possible completions for "
"``command.prefix`` in the current context.  If the completer should not be "
"used in this case, it should return ``None`` or an empty set, which will "
"cause xonsh to move on and try to use the next completer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:98
msgid ""
"Occasionally, completers will need to return a match that does not actually "
"start with ``prefix``.  In this case, a completer should instead return a "
"tuple ``(completions, prefixlength)``, where ``completions`` is the set of "
"appropriate completions, and ``prefixlength`` is the number of characters in "
"``line`` that should be treated as part of the completion."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:105
msgid ""
"Further completion customizations can be made using the ``RichCompletion`` "
"object - see `Advanced Completions`_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:107
msgid ""
"The docstring of a completer should contain a brief description of its "
"functionality, which will be displayed by ``completer list``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:110
msgid ""
"Some simple examples follow.  For real-world examples, see ``xompletions."
"git`` (subprocess-based, uses ``--git-completion-helper``) and ``xonsh."
"completers.man`` (man page parsing with disk cache)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:114
msgid ""
"@ from xonsh.completers.tools import *\n"
"\n"
"@ @contextual_completer\n"
"  def dummy_completer(context):\n"
"      '''\n"
"      Completes everything with options \"lou\" and \"carcolh\",\n"
"      regardless of the value of prefix.\n"
"      '''\n"
"      return {\"lou\", \"carcolh\"}\n"
"\n"
"@ completer add dummy dummy_completer\n"
"@ xzecze<TAB>   # → lou, carcolh\n"
"\n"
"@ @non_exclusive_completer\n"
"  @contextual_completer\n"
"  def nx_dummy_completer(context):\n"
"      '''\n"
"      Like dummy_completer but its results are ADDED to the other "
"completions.\n"
"      '''\n"
"      return {\"lou\", \"carcolh\"}\n"
"\n"
"@ completer add nx_dummy nx_dummy_completer"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:139
msgid ""
"@contextual_completer\n"
"def python_context_completer(context):\n"
"    '''\n"
"    Completes based on the names in the current Python environment\n"
"    '''\n"
"    if context.python:\n"
"        last_name = context.python.prefix.split()[-1]\n"
"        return {i for i in context.python.ctx if i.startswith(last_name)}\n"
"\n"
"@contextual_completer\n"
"def unbeliever_completer(context):\n"
"    '''\n"
"    Replaces \"lou carcolh\" with \"snail\" if tab is pressed after at "
"least\n"
"    typing the \"lou \" part.\n"
"    '''\n"
"    if (\n"
"        # We're completing a command\n"
"        context.command and\n"
"        # We're completing the second argument\n"
"        context.command.arg_index == 1 and\n"
"        # The first argument is 'lou'\n"
"        context.command.args[0].value == 'lou' and\n"
"        # The prefix startswith 'carcolh' (may be empty)\n"
"        'carcolh'.startswith(context.command.prefix)\n"
"    ):\n"
"        return {'snail'}, len('lou ') + len(context.command.prefix)\n"
"\n"
"# Save boilerplate with this helper decorator:\n"
"\n"
"@contextual_command_completer_for(\"lou\")\n"
"def better_unbeliever_completer(command):\n"
"    \"\"\"Like unbeliever_completer but with less boilerplate\"\"\"\n"
"    if command.arg_index == 1 and 'carcolh'.startswith(command.prefix):\n"
"        return {'snail'}, len('lou ') + len(command.prefix)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:176
msgid ""
"To understand how xonsh uses completers and their return values try to set :"
"ref:`$XONSH_COMPLETER_TRACE <xonsh_completer_trace>` to ``True``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:179
msgid ""
"@ $XONSH_COMPLETER_TRACE = True\n"
"@ pip c<TAB>\n"
"TRACE COMPLETIONS: Getting completions with context:\n"
"CompletionContext(command=CommandContext(args=(CommandArg(value='pip', "
"opening_quote='', closing_quote=''),), arg_index=1, prefix='c', suffix='', "
"opening_quote='', closing_quote='', is_after_closing_quote=False, "
"subcmd_opening=''), python=PythonContext('pip c', 5, "
"is_sub_expression=False))\n"
"TRACE COMPLETIONS: Got 3 from exclusive 'pip' for 'c':\n"
"'cache': src=pip, type=exclusive, prefix_len=1, append_space=True\n"
"'check': src=pip, type=exclusive, prefix_len=1, append_space=True\n"
"'config': src=pip, type=exclusive, prefix_len=1, append_space=True"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:190
msgid ""
"The header reads as ``Got <N> from <type> '<name>' for '<prefix>'`` — "
"showing the number of results, the completer's exclusivity, its name, and "
"the prefix the completer tried to match. Each body line shows the completion "
"value, the ``src`` (completer that produced it), the completer ``type`` "
"(``exclusive`` or ``non-exclusive``), and any non-default ``RichCompletion`` "
"attributes (``prefix_len``, ``display``, ``description``, ``append_space``, "
"``close_quote`` — short for ``append_closing_quote`` — ``style``, ``pvd`` — "
"short for ``provider``). The format is grep-friendly, so you can filter by "
"source or type, e.g. ``... | grep 'src=pip'`` or ``... | grep 'type=non-"
"exclusive'``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:202
msgid "Fine-grained origin via ``provider``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:204
msgid ""
"A completer may set a ``provider`` tag on each ``RichCompletion`` to "
"identify the sub-source inside the completer. This is trace-only metadata "
"and does not affect the completion UX."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:208
msgid ""
"For example, the ``base`` completer runs a union of Python names, ``$PATH`` "
"executables, aliases, and file paths. When you type the first argument and "
"hit ``<TAB>``, the trace distinguishes them:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:212
msgid ""
"@ aliases['qwe-xonsh'] = 'echo'\n"
"@ xonsh<TAB>\n"
"TRACE COMPLETIONS: Got 2 from exclusive 'base' for 'xonsh':\n"
"'qwe-xonsh ': src=base, pvd='alias', type=exclusive, prefix_len=5, "
"append_space=True\n"
"'xonsh ': src=base, pvd='command', type=exclusive, prefix_len=5, "
"append_space=True"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:220
msgid ""
"Completers that are invoked but return no usable matches are also reported, "
"so you can see the full decision path:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:223
msgid ""
"TRACE COMPLETIONS: Got 0 from non-exclusive 'environment_vars' for 'xonsh'.\n"
"TRACE COMPLETIONS: Got 0 from exclusive 'bash' for 'xonsh'.\n"
"TRACE COMPLETIONS: Got 3 from exclusive 'path' for './do':\n"
"..."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:230
msgid ""
"This way you can see immediately that ``qwe-xonsh`` comes from an alias "
"while ``xonsh`` is a real executable on ``$PATH``. Built-in providers: "
"``alias``, ``command``, ``python``, ``path``, plus the xompletion module "
"name (e.g. ``pip``, ``gh``) for completions produced by the ``xompleter``. "
"Custom completers may set any string they like."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:237
msgid "Setting ``provider`` in your own completer"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:239
msgid ""
"There are two ways to tag your completions, depending on whether you want "
"per-item control or want to label the whole result at once."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:242
msgid ""
"**Per-item**: pass ``provider=...`` directly to each ``RichCompletion``. "
"This is most useful when a single completer has internal branches and you "
"want to distinguish them in trace:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:246
msgid ""
"from xonsh.completers.tools import (\n"
"    RichCompletion,\n"
"    contextual_command_completer_for,\n"
")\n"
"\n"
"@contextual_command_completer_for(\"deploy\")\n"
"def complete_deploy(command):\n"
"    \"\"\"Complete ``deploy <env>`` with fast- and slow-paths tagged.\"\"\"\n"
"    cached = {\"prod\", \"staging\"}       # e.g. loaded from a local cache\n"
"    remote = {\"dev-42\", \"dev-43\"}      # e.g. hit an HTTP endpoint\n"
"\n"
"    for env in cached:\n"
"        if env.startswith(command.prefix):\n"
"            yield RichCompletion(env, provider=\"deploy:cache\")\n"
"    for env in remote:\n"
"        if env.startswith(command.prefix):\n"
"            yield RichCompletion(env, provider=\"deploy:remote\")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:266
msgid "After ``completer add deploy complete_deploy`` the trace will show:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:268
msgid ""
"TRACE COMPLETIONS: Got 3 from exclusive 'deploy' for 'd':\n"
"'dev-42': src=deploy, pvd='deploy:remote', type=exclusive, prefix_len=1\n"
"'dev-43': src=deploy, pvd='deploy:remote', type=exclusive, prefix_len=1\n"
"'staging': src=deploy, pvd='deploy:cache', type=exclusive, prefix_len=1"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:275
msgid ""
"**Whole-result**: wrap the completer's return value with ``tag_provider``. "
"This preserves the return shape (``None``, iterable, or ``(comps, lprefix)`` "
"tuple) and only tags completions that do not already carry their own "
"``provider``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:280
msgid ""
"from xonsh.completers.tools import (\n"
"    contextual_command_completer_for,\n"
"    tag_provider,\n"
")\n"
"\n"
"@contextual_command_completer_for(\"mycmd\")\n"
"def complete_mycmd(command):\n"
"    \"\"\"Completer whose entire output is tagged ``provider='mycmd'``."
"\"\"\"\n"
"    results = {\"start\", \"stop\", \"status\"}\n"
"    return tag_provider(results, \"mycmd\")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:293
msgid ""
"``tag_provider`` is also what the built-in ``xompleter`` uses under the hood "
"to label each xompletion module's output — so any ``RichCompletion`` that "
"*already* specifies its own ``provider`` is kept intact and won't be "
"overwritten by the outer tag."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:301
msgid "Registering a Completer"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:303
msgid ""
"Once you have created a completion function, you can add it to the list of "
"active completers via the ``completer add`` command or ``xonsh.completers."
"completer.add_one_completer`` function::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:306
msgid ""
"Usage:\n"
"    completer add NAME FUNC [POS]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:309
msgid "``NAME`` is a unique name to use in the listing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:311
msgid "``FUNC`` is the name of a completer function to use."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:313
msgid ""
"``POS`` (optional) is a position into the list of completers at which the "
"new completer should be added.  It can be one of the following values:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:315
msgid ""
"``\"start\"`` indicates that the completer should be added to the start of "
"the list of completers ("
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:316
msgid "it should be run before all other exclusive completers)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:317
msgid ""
"``\"end\"`` indicates that the completer should be added to the end of the "
"list of completers (it should be run after all others)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:318
msgid ""
"``\">KEY\"``, where ``KEY`` is a pre-existing name, indicates that this "
"should be added after the completer named ``KEY``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:319
msgid ""
"``\"<KEY\"``, where ``KEY`` is a pre-existing name, indicates that this "
"should be added before the completer named ``KEY``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:321
msgid "If ``POS`` is not provided, it defaults to ``\"start\"``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:323
msgid ""
"It is also possible to manipulate ``__xonsh__.completers`` directly, but "
"this is the preferred method."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:327
msgid "Removing a Completer"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:329
msgid ""
"To remove a completer from the list of active completers, run ``completer "
"remove NAME``, where ``NAME`` is the unique identifier associated with the "
"completer you wish to remove."
msgstr ""

