msgid "Advanced Completions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:336
msgid ""
"To provide further control over the completion, a completer can return a :"
"class:`RichCompletion <xonsh.completers.tools.RichCompletion>` object. Using "
"this class, you can:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:339
msgid "Provide a specific prefix length per completion (via ``prefix_len``)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:340
msgid ""
"Control how the completion looks in prompt-toolkit (via ``display``, "
"``description`` and ``style``) -"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:341
msgid "use the ``jedi`` xontrib to see it in action."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:342
msgid "Append a space after the completion (``append_space=True``)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:346
msgid "Completing Closed String Literals"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:347
msgid ""
"When the cursor is appending to a closed string literal (i.e. cursor at the "
"end of ``ls \"/usr/\"``), the following happens:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:349
msgid "The closing quote will be appended to all completions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:350
msgid ""
"I.e the completion ``/usr/bin`` will turn into ``/usr/bin\"``. To prevent "
"this behavior, a completer can return a ``RichCompletion`` with "
"``append_closing_quote=False``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:352
msgid "If not specified, lprefix will cover the closing prefix."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:353
msgid ""
"I.e for ``ls \"/usr/\"``, the default lprefix will be 6 to include the "
"closing quote. To prevent this behavior, a completer can return a different "
"lprefix or specify it inside ``RichCompletion``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:356
msgid ""
"So if you want to change/remove the quotes from a string, the following "
"completer can be written:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:358
msgid ""
"@contextual_command_completer\n"
"def remove_quotes(command):\n"
"    \"\"\"\n"
"    Return a completer that will remove the quotes, i.e:\n"
"    which \"python\"<TAB> -> which python\n"
"    echo \"hi<TAB> -> echo hi\n"
"    ls \"file with spaces\"<TAB> -> ls file with spaces\n"
"    \"\"\"\n"
"    raw_prefix_len = len(command.raw_prefix)  # this includes the closing "
"quote if it exists\n"
"    return {RichCompletion(command.prefix, prefix_len=raw_prefix_len, "
"append_closing_quote=False)}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:373
msgid "Completing Aliases"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:375
msgid ""
"You can attach a custom completer to a function alias using the ``@aliases."
"completer`` decorator:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:378
msgid ""
"def _complete_hello(command, alias):\n"
"    return {'world', 'there', 'xonsh'}\n"
"\n"
"@aliases.register\n"
"@aliases.completer(_complete_hello)\n"
"def _hello(args):\n"
"    echo @(args)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:388
msgid "Now ``hello <TAB>`` will suggest ``world``, ``there``, and ``xonsh``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:390
msgid "You can also set the ``xonsh_complete`` attribute manually:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:392
msgid ""
"def _hello(args):\n"
"    echo @(args)\n"
"\n"
"_hello.xonsh_complete = lambda *a, **kw: {'world', 'there', 'xonsh'}\n"
"aliases['hello'] = _hello"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:400
msgid "The completer function receives two keyword arguments:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:402
msgid ""
"``command``: the :class:`CommandContext <xonsh.parsers.completion_context."
"CommandContext>` for the current completion"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:403
msgid "``alias``: the resolved alias object"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:406
msgid "Command Completers (xompletions)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:408
msgid ""
"xonsh includes a package called ``xompletions`` that provides tab-"
"completions for specific commands like ``pip``, ``gh``, ``cd``, etc. Each "
"command gets its own Python module inside the ``xompletions/`` directory."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:412
msgid "How it works:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:414
msgid ""
"When the user presses TAB, the ``xompleter`` completer (registered as "
"``complete_xompletions``) extracts the command name from ``args[0]``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:416
msgid ""
"It looks for a matching module in ``xompletions/`` — first by exact name, "
"then by regex patterns."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:417
msgid "If found, it calls the module's ``xonsh_complete(ctx)`` function."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:418
msgid ""
"The function returns completions or ``None`` (to let the next completer "
"handle it)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:421
msgid "Creating a command completer"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:423
msgid ""
"To create a completer for a command, place a Python file named after the "
"command in any directory listed in ``$XONSH_COMPLETER_DIRS``. The file must "
"contain a ``xonsh_complete`` function:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:427
msgid ""
"# ~/.config/xonsh/completers/mycmd.py\n"
"from xonsh.parsers.completion_context import CommandContext\n"
"\n"
"def xonsh_complete(ctx: CommandContext):\n"
"    \"\"\"Completes mycmd subcommands.\"\"\"\n"
"    if ctx.arg_index == 1:\n"
"        return {'start', 'stop', 'status'}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:437
msgid "$XONSH_COMPLETER_DIRS = [\"~/.config/xonsh/completers\"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:441
msgid "Now ``mycmd <TAB>`` will suggest ``start``, ``stop``, and ``status``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:443
msgid ""
"xonsh also ships built-in completers in the ``xompletions/`` package (for "
"``pip``, ``gh``, ``cd``, etc.)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:446
msgid "Handling command name variants with ``wrap``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:448
msgid ""
"The file name must match the command name exactly (``gh.py`` for ``gh``). On "
"Windows, extensions like ``.exe`` are stripped automatically via "
"``$PATHEXT``, so ``gh.exe`` will find ``gh.py``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:452
msgid ""
"However, if a command has other name variants (e.g. ``pip3.11``, "
"``python3.12``), the exact file name won't match. For these cases, you can "
"register regex patterns from your :doc:`xonsh RC <xonshrc>` or a xontrib:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:456
msgid ""
"from xonsh.completers.commands import complete_xompletions as xmp\n"
"xmp.wrap(r\"\\bmycmd(?:\\d)*$\", \"mycmd\")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:461
msgid ""
"This maps ``mycmd``, ``mycmd2``, ``mycmd3`` etc. to the ``mycmd`` completer "
"module."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:463
msgid ""
"xonsh ships with built-in patterns for ``pip`` (covers ``xpip``, "
"``pip3.11``, ``pip.exe``) and ``python`` (covers ``python3``, "
"``python3.12``, ``python.exe``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:467
msgid "Completing ``python -m <module>``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:469
msgid ""
"When an alias resolves to ``python -m <module>`` (e.g. ``xpip`` → ``python -"
"m pip``), xonsh uses the ``xompletions/python.py`` completer to delegate to "
"the module's completer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:472
msgid ""
"The mapping is stored in ``PYTHON_MODULE_COMPLETERS`` and can be extended "
"from your :doc:`xonsh RC <xonshrc>`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:474
msgid ""
"from xompletions.python import PYTHON_MODULE_COMPLETERS\n"
"\n"
"# Simple completer with static options\n"
"def _complete_mytool(ctx, module_arg_index):\n"
"    return {'start', 'stop', 'status'}\n"
"\n"
"PYTHON_MODULE_COMPLETERS['mytool'] = _complete_mytool"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:484
msgid ""
"Now ``python -m mytool <TAB>`` will suggest ``start``, ``stop``, and "
"``status``. This also works through aliases:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:487
msgid ""
"aliases['mt'] = ['python', '-m', 'mytool']\n"
"mt <TAB>  # completes with start, stop, status"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:492
msgid ""
"For modules that use the `argcomplete <https://github.com/kislyuk/"
"argcomplete>`_ protocol, a ready-made helper is available:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:495
msgid ""
"from xompletions.python import PYTHON_MODULE_COMPLETERS, "
"_complete_argcomplete\n"
"\n"
"PYTHON_MODULE_COMPLETERS['my_argcomplete_tool'] = _complete_argcomplete"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:503
msgid "Shortcut Path Completion"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:505
msgid ""
"When typing long paths, xonsh can complete each segment from a one- or two-"
"character hint instead of the full directory name — every slash-separated "
"segment is matched as a *subsequence* of an actual entry, similar to fish "
"and zsh."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:510
msgid ""
"@ cd /u/lo/b<TAB>             # → cd /usr/local/bin/\n"
"@ cat ~/p/g/x/READ<TAB>       # → cat ~/Projects/git/xonsh/README.rst"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:515
msgid ""
"Hits are tried in order: exact prefix → substring → subsequence → fuzzy "
"(Levenshtein), so a regular full prefix still wins when one is available. "
"Both subsequence and fuzzy matching are on by default and can be turned off "
"via ``$SUBSEQUENCE_PATH_COMPLETION = False`` or ``$FUZZY_PATH_COMPLETION = "
"False`` in your :doc:`xonsh RC <xonshrc>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:521
msgid ""
"Path completion is also case-insensitive on POSIX — typing ``/USR/lo<TAB>`` "
"resolves to ``/usr/local/`` even on case-sensitive filesystems like ext4."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:527
msgid "Emoji & Symbols"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:529
msgid ""
"Need a 🐈 in your commit message? xonsh has a built-in emoji completer. It "
"is disabled by default. To enable, set the trigger prefixes:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:532
msgid ""
"@ $XONSH_COMPLETER_EMOJI_PREFIX = '::'\n"
"@ $XONSH_COMPLETER_SYMBOLS_PREFIX = ':::'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:537
msgid ""
"Then type ``::`` followed by a keyword and press TAB to search for colorful "
"emoji:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:540
msgid ""
"echo \"great job ::fire<TAB>\"   →  echo \"great job 🔥\"\n"
"echo \"::cat<TAB>\"              →  echo \"🐈\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:545
msgid "For classic unicode symbols (arrows, math, stars), use ``:::``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:547
msgid ""
"echo \":::arrow<TAB>\"  →  echo \"→\"\n"
"echo \":::star<TAB>\"   →  echo \"★\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:552
msgid ""
"Set ``$XONSH_COMPLETER_EMOJI_PREFIX`` or ``$XONSH_COMPLETER_SYMBOLS_PREFIX`` "
"to ``None`` to disable the corresponding completer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:559
msgid "Selecting a tab completion result"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:561
msgid ""
"In the ``prompt_toolkit`` shell, you can cycle through possible tab-"
"completion results using the TAB key and use ENTER to select the completion "
"you want. By default, ENTER will also execute the current line. If you would "
"prefer to not automatically execute the line (say, if you're constructing a "
"long pathname), you can set"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:567
msgid "$COMPLETIONS_CONFIRM = True"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:571
msgid "in your :doc:`xonsh RC <xonshrc>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:573
msgid ""
"By default, TABs cycle through the full list. Set ``$COMPLETION_MODE = "
"\"menu-complete\"`` to instead insert the first whole completion on the "
"first TAB and cycle through the rest on further TABs. This is only one of "
"many knobs; see `Tab Completion Behavior <https://xon.sh/envvars.html#tab-"
"completion-behavior>`_ for the full list (display style, menu rows, "
"threading, trace output, and more)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:582
msgid "Man Page Completer"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:584
msgid ""
"When no dedicated completer exists for a command, xonsh falls back to "
"parsing the command's **man page** to extract option names (``-v``, ``--"
"verbose``, etc.). This works automatically for most CLI tools."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:588
msgid ""
"For commands that use per-subcommand man pages (``docker-run``, ``cargo-"
"build``, ``systemctl-start``, etc.), xonsh tries the hyphenated form ``man "
"<cmd>-<subcmd>`` first and falls back to ``man <cmd>``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:592
msgid ""
"Parsed options are cached on disk under ``$XONSH_DATA_DIR/"
"generated_completions/man/``. The cache is invalidated automatically when "
"the man page file is updated (e.g. after a package upgrade). To force a "
"refresh — for example, after upgrading xonsh itself with an improved parser "
"— clear the cache:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:598
msgid "@ rm $XONSH_DATA_DIR/generated_completions/man/*"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:603
msgid "Installing man pages for tools"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:605
msgid ""
"Some tools (Docker, Podman, etc.) ship man pages in a separate package. If "
"``docker run -<TAB>`` returns no completions, the man page may not be "
"installed. On macOS with Homebrew:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:609
msgid "$ brew install docker"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:613
msgid ""
"On Linux, man pages are usually part of the main package, but some "
"distributions split them out (e.g. ``docker-doc`` on Debian/Ubuntu)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:616
msgid "You can verify whether a man page is available:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:618
msgid "$ man -w docker-run"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:622
msgid ""
"If this prints a path, completions will work. If it prints an error, install "
"the corresponding package."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:627
msgid "Legacy Completers Support"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:629
msgid ""
"Before completion context was introduced, xonsh had a different readline-"
"like completion API. While this legacy API is not recommended, xonsh still "
"supports it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:633
msgid ""
"The legacy completers are less robust than the contextual system in many "
"situations, for example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:635
msgid "``ls $(which<TAB>`` completes with the prefix ``$(which``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:637
msgid ""
"``ls 'a file<TAB>`` completes with the prefix ``file`` (instead of ``a "
"file``)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:639
msgid ""
"See `Completion Context PR <https://github.com/xonsh/xonsh/pull/4017>`_ for "
"more information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:641
msgid ""
"Legacy completers are python functions that aren't marked by "
"``@contextual_completer`` and receive the following arguments:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:643
msgid ""
"``prefix``: the string to be matched (the last whitespace-separated token in "
"the current line)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:644
msgid "``line``: a string representing the entire current line"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:645
msgid "``begidx``: the index at which ``prefix`` starts in ``line``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:646
msgid "``endidx``: the length of the ``prefix`` in ``line``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:647
msgid ""
"``ctx``: the current Python environment, as a dictionary mapping names to "
"values"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../completers.rst:649
msgid ""
"Their return value can be any of the variations of the contextual "
"completers'."
msgstr ""
